WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.376 --> 00:00:02.727
These are some of our free tools on offer.

2
00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:05.609
The calculator prices the dependency.

3
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.431
The extraction instrument surfaces the hidden logic.

4
00:00:09.832 --> 00:00:13.214
The map tests whether it survives a buying committee.

5
00:00:15.498 --> 00:00:17.099
Each tool is useful.

6
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.320
None is the system.

7
00:00:19.781 --> 00:00:23.503
The system makes that logic transferable across the company.

8
00:00:23.884 --> 00:00:26.726
Different doors, same question.

9
00:00:26.886 --> 00:00:31.970
Can the company carry the founder's selling logic without waiting for the founder?

10
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.932
Think about the late stage deal waiting for you.

11
00:00:35.573 --> 00:00:37.454
The team knows the product.

12
00:00:37.474 --> 00:00:38.835
They have the deck.

13
00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:43.488
But the case doesn't close until you make it land.

14
00:00:44.354 --> 00:00:47.497
That dependence is not a character flaw.

15
00:00:47.898 --> 00:00:49.880
It's usually how the company got here.

16
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.385
That's how you raise capital, got your first customers, hired great talent.

17
00:00:54.766 --> 00:00:57.852
But the next stage asks you to make

18
00:00:57.852 --> 00:01:02.524
what you've learned, that secret sauce, transferable.

19
00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:08.225
Can your team articulate your value in a way that

20
00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:12.070
moves a skeptical buyer without you in the room?

21
00:01:13.291 --> 00:01:20.234
That's the Room Test And if the answer is no, we call the condition StoryLock The

22
00:01:20.234 --> 00:01:28.463
hire, the agency, the GTM stack, the AI may all be capable, but they can't carry logic they were

23
00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:29.673
never given.

24
00:01:30.134 --> 00:01:33.304
It's not about talent, it's a transfer problem.

25
00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:34.874
We do four things.

26
00:01:35.175 --> 00:01:40.777
Extract, codify, deploy, and tune.

27
00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:48.390
Extract is the three hour Rumble a challenging, immersive experience where we pull out the

28
00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:53.342
decisions, stories, instincts, and reflexes you use under pressure.

29
00:01:53.882 --> 00:02:00.145
Codify turns those into the six-piece source code and strategic sales narrative.

30
00:02:01.045 --> 00:02:03.476
Deploy installs that logic.

31
00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:09.532
Through team fluency and the selected customer-facing surfaces, we agree to at kickoff.

32
00:02:09.532 --> 00:02:11.753
Not every channel all at once.

33
00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:17.939
Tune means watching where the logic holds and testing what improves it.

34
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.702
Your part is concentrated.

35
00:02:20.702 --> 00:02:25.082
The founder joins the Rumble A small team joins focused

36
00:02:25.082 --> 00:02:29.780
reviews, and we do the extraction, build, and installation.

37
00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.235
If you're the operator trying to build one source of truth inside the

38
00:02:34.235 --> 00:02:37.617
org, you can help solve the same condition from the other side.

39
00:02:37.658 --> 00:02:40.121
But the founder still participates.

40
00:02:40.121 --> 00:02:41.302
That's essential.

41
00:02:41.302 --> 00:02:45.766
What you're seeing is an illustrative composite, not a client result.

42
00:02:46.448 --> 00:02:52.334
The real work is competitively sensitive, so I'm showing you the exact pattern, not published outcomes.

43
00:02:55.962 --> 00:02:57.983
Column one is the channel.

44
00:02:58.664 --> 00:03:05.526
The website says "We are a modern end to end platform that helps enterprise teams streamline

45
00:03:05.526 --> 00:03:10.672
operations and drive efficiency at scale." Column two is the founder.

46
00:03:11.012 --> 00:03:13.173
Same company, same idea.

47
00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:15.635
"Nobody's operations are broken.

48
00:03:15.655 --> 00:03:19.518
What's broken is that no two systems agree on what happened.

49
00:03:19.518 --> 00:03:26.162
So every decision starts with an argument about the data." Those are not the same story.

50
00:03:27.172 --> 00:03:28.908
Column three codifies it.

51
00:03:29.073 --> 00:03:31.956
"We end the argument about whose numbers are right."

52
00:03:33.454 --> 00:03:36.460
This composite demonstrates the transformation pattern.

53
00:03:36.642 --> 00:03:40.820
The founder's reasoning becomes usable by somebody else under pressure.

54
00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:50.309
The source code captures six pieces reason you exist, your wedge,

55
00:03:50.309 --> 00:03:56.277
the stakes, your origin, proof pattern, primary objection.

56
00:03:57.218 --> 00:04:02.071
The argument stays stable while proof and entry point

57
00:04:02.071 --> 00:04:06.924
change for the CFO, evaluator, user, and procurement.

58
00:04:06.924 --> 00:04:10.066
If we stopped here, you'd have a useful document.

59
00:04:11.106 --> 00:04:14.487
The problem is that it starts going stale the day you get it.

60
00:04:14.688 --> 00:04:19.209
People change, markets move, channels drift.

61
00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.830
So we don't stop at the documentation.

62
00:04:22.231 --> 00:04:26.892
Those six pieces become the operating logic the system runs on.

63
00:04:27.252 --> 00:04:32.035
That's the difference between a resource and an installed operating system.

64
00:04:32.035 --> 00:04:34.616
The six pieces are the source code.

65
00:04:35.096 --> 00:04:39.598
We install them into the selected places your team actually sells.

66
00:04:40.196 --> 00:04:43.449
But a document is not installed just because it exists.

67
00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:48.009
Before we call it installed, we rerun the room test

68
00:04:48.009 --> 00:04:52.598
with one person each from sales, marketing, and product.

69
00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:56.682
Can they carry the argument without the founder?

70
00:05:01.796 --> 00:05:06.329
This is a measurement layer, not another dashboard your reps have to adopt.

71
00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.132
It gives us a disciplined way to ask where the

72
00:05:10.132 --> 00:05:14.135
logic held, where it drifted, and what to test next.

73
00:05:14.715 --> 00:05:16.656
Here's a sample hypothesis.

74
00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:23.742
Founder-led posts will outperform product posts on qualified engagement and pipeline contribution.

75
00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:29.646
Run the test for four weeks, then keep, change, or kill the cadence.

76
00:05:33.752 --> 00:05:40.660
Here's the ownership proof, the source, talk tracks, objection logic, deployment assets,

77
00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.506
and team enablement material in your environment.

78
00:05:45.287 --> 00:05:48.768
The first three jobs happen during the 75 day install.

79
00:05:48.828 --> 00:05:53.350
After that, you choose whether your team tunes it or we do.

80
00:05:53.851 --> 00:05:57.012
The system stays, but we don't have to.

81
00:05:57.012 --> 00:05:59.993
At day 30, we review agreed milestones.

82
00:06:00.233 --> 00:06:02.634
If you don't see real value, stop.

83
00:06:02.658 --> 00:06:04.759
And pay only for completed work.

84
00:06:05.741 --> 00:06:09.284
At day 75, the installed system is all yours.

85
00:06:09.745 --> 00:06:13.815
The goal is not to remove your best work, it's to remove the

86
00:06:13.815 --> 00:06:17.952
rescue work you have to do because the team lacks the system.

87
00:06:18.853 --> 00:06:23.214
We track qualified, late stage deals requiring the founder,

88
00:06:23.214 --> 00:06:26.540
with win rate and deal quality as guardrails.

89
00:06:26.941 --> 00:06:28.526
Zero is not the goal.

90
00:06:28.526 --> 00:06:34.890
The goal is to pare it down to the deals where your involvement is a choice, not a rescue.

91
00:06:35.271 --> 00:06:37.252
You don't want to stop being involved.

92
00:06:37.432 --> 00:06:40.073
You want to stop being indispensable.

93
00:06:40.394 --> 00:06:42.135
Involved is a choice.

94
00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:44.677
Indispensable is a ceiling.

95
00:06:45.257 --> 00:06:46.738
And this is the point.

96
00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:50.533
The architecture you just experienced is what we install so

97
00:06:50.533 --> 00:06:54.063
your team can carry the argument without waiting for you.

98
00:06:55.224 --> 00:06:56.705
Still diagnosing?

99
00:06:57.025 --> 00:06:58.486
Quantify the cost.

100
00:06:58.764 --> 00:07:01.656
Surface the logic, or map a buying committee.

101
00:07:01.656 --> 00:07:03.317
None requires a call.

102
00:07:04.138 --> 00:07:08.060
If you already know the problem is structural, book the 30-minute diagnostic.

103
00:07:08.221 --> 00:07:10.522
That choice requests a conversation.

104
00:07:10.983 --> 00:07:15.305
Our system will compare your public story with your channels.

105
00:07:15.686 --> 00:07:19.348
We'll walk the scorecard live together, and you keep it.

106
00:07:20.209 --> 00:07:27.134
This is for founder-led, complex sale B2B companies, typically at $3 to $50 million in ARR.

107
00:07:27.397 --> 00:07:30.230
We're looking for PMF and momentum.

108
00:07:31.393 --> 00:07:34.356
If the founder won't participate, don't book.

109
00:07:34.898 --> 00:07:39.784
If the problem's not structural or we're not the right fit, I'll be quick to tell you.
